course: everyday
lesson: 355
title: Cheek to grown-ups and saying sorry
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Liam's friends in Dublin dared him to call the lollipop lady a rude name as they crossed. He did, and they all ran off laughing. She told the school, and Liam had to write her a letter. The letter was done, but the bad feeling was not: every morning he crossed with his eyes on the ground.
explain: On Saturday the family went to confession. Liam whispered «Bless me, Father, for I have sinned» and told the priest about the name. The priest listened, said that God forgives him, gave him a small penance — a short prayer and one kind thing for the lollipop lady — and said «Now go and say sorry to her face». Liam came out feeling light, as if he had put down a heavy bag. On Monday he said sorry to her in person and brought her a card; she smiled and said «Mind the road, pet».
explain: Confession lifted the weight, but it did not replace the sorry to the person he had hurt; he did both. A dare does not make rude words fine — the one who says them chooses to.
explain: Rudeness to adults comes in many forms: shouting at the coach, mocking an accent, muttering, slamming a door. Each one needs the same fix: own it, say sorry, change the words next time. When Ben called his uncle a name after his uncle teased him, Dad sent Ben to the car for five minutes; when Ben came back and said sorry, his uncle said sorry too for the teasing.
fact: A dare does not make a rude act acceptable; the person who does it is still responsible.
fact: In confession, a Catholic or Anglican tells their sins to a priest, who gives God's forgiveness (absolution) and often a small penance.
fact: Many people feel real relief after confession, like a weight lifted.
fact: Confession does not replace saying sorry and putting things right with the person you hurt.
fact: Shouting at a referee or coach does not change the decision, and can get you benched or sent off.
fact: Muttering rude words under your breath is still being rude if the other person hears.
fact: An accent shows where someone grew up or learned the language; mocking it is unkind.
fact: Slamming a door ends a talk but does not solve the problem.
fact: When both people did something wrong, two apologies often fix it best.
fact: Saying sorry quickly, and explaining you were upset, usually makes forgiveness easier.
exercise: Why did Liam call the lollipop lady a rude name? | His friends dared him | She had been rude to him first | He did not know she could hear | He was bored at the crossing
exercise: Does a dare make a rude act fine? | No, the one who does it is still responsible | Yes, the blame goes to the friends who dared him | Yes, if everyone laughs | Only if it is a double dare
exercise: After writing the letter, why did Liam still feel bad? | He had not yet said sorry to her face | The letter was badly spelt | He wanted to be dared again | He had lost his pen
exercise: In confession, what did the priest give Liam? | God's forgiveness and a small penance | A week of detention | A note for his teacher | A new set of rules for school
exercise: How did Liam feel after confession? | Light, as if a weight had gone | Worse than before | Angry with the priest | Nothing changed
exercise: Why did the priest still send Liam to say sorry in person? | Confession does not replace putting it right with her | The priest did not believe that Liam was really sorry | The lollipop lady is the priest's friend | Liam had to confess again first
exercise: Josh shouted at the coach for taking him off. What was the consequence? | He was benched for the next game | He was put back on at once | The coach came over and said sorry to him in front of everyone | He became team captain
exercise: Does shouting at a coach change the decision? | No, it makes it worse | Yes, if you shout loudly enough | Yes, if the team agrees | Only if it is in the final
exercise: Some pupils laughed at a supply teacher's accent. What would put it right? | Saying sorry and telling her she taught well | Saying the accent really was funny | Asking for a different teacher | Copying the accent again, but more kindly this time
exercise: Ben called his uncle a name after his uncle teased him. Dad sent Ben to the car for five minutes. What happened when Ben said sorry? | His uncle said sorry too for teasing | His uncle teased him even more about it at dinner | Nobody spoke to Ben again | His uncle took his present back
exercise: What does Ben's story show? | Two wrongs don't make a right, but two sorrys help | Teasing someone first makes name-calling them back fine | Uncles are never wrong | Only children need to apologise
exercise: Molly slammed the door in Dad's face. He knocked and said «we need to talk». What was the wise next step? | Open the door, say sorry and talk | Push a chair against the door | Turn the music up loud until Dad gives up and goes away | Wait until Dad forgets
exercise: Ella muttered rude things under her breath when asked to tidy. Mum heard every word. What is true? | Muttering is still rude | Muttering does not count | Mums cannot hear mutters | It is fine if you tidy anyway
exercise: Sam said «shut up» to his teacher by mistake when he was cross. What helped most? | Saying sorry soon and explaining he was upset | Saying nothing and hoping she forgets | Blaming the boy who annoyed him | Saying it was a joke
exercise: Liam's friend says «you went to confession, so you don't have to say sorry to her now». What is true? | He still needs to say sorry to her in person | Confession cancels every sorry you would ever have to say | Priests say sorry for you | Sorry is only for grown-ups
exercise: A boy says the wrong word to his aunt on the phone and she tells Mum. He loses phone time. What would put it right? | Calling her back and saying sorry | Texting her a funny picture | Never calling her again | Saying the phone line was bad
test: «dare ya to call the lollipop lady a old cow». What's the best answer? | No, she keeps us safe; I'm not doing that | OK, but only if you do the dare as well, straight after | OK, quietly so she can't hear | Yes, a dare is a dare
test: Priest: «Is there anything else troubling you?» You called someone a name last week. Best answer? | Tell him about the name too | Say no and leave the room quickly | Say someone else in your class did it | Ask him what he did last week
test: «Father said I'm forgiven, so I don't need to say sorry to her, sorted». Is that right? | No, you still say sorry to the one you hurt | Yes, forgiveness from Father covers everything, sorted | Yes, priests pass it on | Only if she asks
test: Coach takes u off at half time, u scream «this is a joke!». Next week you're on the bench. Why? | Shouting at the coach has consequences | The coach just hates you | Benches are random | Half time is the rule
test: supply teacher says «ze homework» and the back row cracks up. You laughed too. Best fix? | Say sorry to her and treat her with respect | Laugh louder to fit in | Say the others in the back row started it, not you | Ask the head for a new teacher
test: Uncle: «Bit podgy these days, eh?» You call him a rude name back. Which is true? | Both did wrong; two sorrys can fix it | Only he did wrong, because he started it with «podgy» | Only you did wrong | Nobody did wrong
test: muttered «stupid tidy-up» last week and mum didn't hear. «it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, it's still rude, and next time she may hear | Yes, if she didn't hear it then it doesn't really count | Yes, muttering never counts | Only on weekends
test: slammed my door, dad's outside going «can we talk?». I'm still raging. Best move? | Take a breath, then open and talk | Hold the door shut all night until he gives up | Shout «go away» | Climb out the window
test: blurted «shut up» at Miss Ross, the whole class went «oooh». What now? | Say sorry quickly and explain you were upset | Pretend it was someone else | Say it again louder so you look tough in front of everyone | Walk out of class
test: lollipop lady told the school about u and u had to write a letter. Is the letter enough? | Saying sorry to her face makes it complete | Yes, a letter is always enough and you never need to see her | No, you need to write ten | She will not read it anyway
test: You went to confession and feel light again. Your little brother asks «so it's like it never happened?». Best answer? | I'm forgiven, but I still have to put it right | Yes, nothing happened | Yes, so I can do the same thing again and go back next week | No, confession does nothing
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a dare does not excuse a discourteous act. | Responsibility rests with the person who performs the act | Dares are prohibited by school regulations and therefore excuse nothing | Dares transfer responsibility to the challenger | Discourtesy is excused only when unintended
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between confession and the making of amends? | Confession brings forgiveness but does not replace an apology to the person wronged | Confession removes any obligation towards the person wronged | Amends are required only in those cases where confession has been refused | Confession and amends are mutually exclusive
exam: It is given that a pupil, upon removal from play by a coach, shouted at the coach. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The protest does not alter the decision and may incur a sanction | The protest obliges the coach to reverse the decision without delay | The protest is a recognised appeal procedure | The protest is without consequence
exam: State the outcome most commonly associated with prompt apology following an angry remark to a teacher. | Forgiveness is more readily obtained | The remark is entered on a permanent record | The pupil is automatically excluded from school | The teacher is required to apologise in turn
